# The server

Every page of the site reaches the browser as finished HTML, so a reader — and a crawler — sees the page before the client boots. Beside the page travels the model it was rendered from, inlined for hydration to read. The server that sends it is a small Effect HTTP server started from a Bun runtime edge.

Rendering every route matters because the site navigates by ordinary links. A nav entry carries a real `href`, so a click leaves the page and asks the server for the next one, and a reader who reloads or arrives from outside asks for a route directly. A server that answered only the landing would give every other page a not-found.

There is one server. It reads the shell and the assets from `dist` and serves the bundle beside them. Development runs this same file against that same directory, so nothing about how a page arrives differs between a developer's machine and a reader's. What differs is only who writes `dist`: a build, or a build left watching.

That is worth more than it sounds. A server that renders one way and is developed another agrees with itself only in what a page finally says, and disagrees in everything about how the page arrives — which stylesheet, in which order, before or after the body is visible. Running one path twice settles all of it at once.

A page depends on nothing but its address and the build: the shell the build wrote, the documentation and the book. So the site renders its pages when it is built rather than when a reader asks. The build renders every address the site answers and keeps each page as a file in `dist`, and the server answers a request for a page by reading its file. The chapter *Rendering every page ahead* tells how the build does it.

The server can still render a page itself, and does whenever `dist` holds no page rendered for the build it serves. That is the case in development, where the client is rebuilt on every change and every rebuild is a new build. Both paths render through the same functions over the same model, so a page rendered ahead and a page rendered on demand are the same page.

The rendering lives in a module of its own, which the server and the build share. This chapter tells that module first, then the server.

The server stays clear of the client runtime. It renders through Foldkit's server module, which runs the view once over a model and writes the result into the shell, and it never starts the runtime the browser boots. The browser hydrates and takes over.

## The page

The page starts from the built shell and gains three things: the rendered body in place of the empty `#root`, the route's own title in place of the shell's, and, beside the body, the model it was rendered from, which the client reads back to hydrate.

The seed is the site at rest, and every page starts from it — no chapter opened, no section jumped to, the example standing at its first frame, and no slice of the book. It names the landing's route because a model must name one; each page then overrides that single field with its own.

The pill and the title bar both name the latest release of Loom, and the seed carries it as the workspace's own version, which the tangle writes in. A release is always a commit that bumps that version, and the site is built again from the same commit. So the version a page names is the release its build ships, from the moment the page goes out. A page rendered ahead could not ask npm instead. The site is built before the release reaches npm, so npm would still name the release before it.

{{ version = ${version} }}

=>

export const seed: Model = {
  route: 'home',
  chapter: '',
  drawerOpen: false,
  runtime: 'bun',
  searchOpen: false,
  caret: 0,
  searching: false,
  found: [],
  activeSection: '',
  player: { struck: 0, beat: 0, playing: false, output: Option.none(), full: false },
  version: '::[version]',
  query: '',
  exact: false,
  focus: 0,
  copied: '',
  book: Option.none(),
}

## Loom's own book

The source page draws Loom's own book, and the server is where the book lives. The site's build weaves `corpus/book.loom` to `source.woven.json` beside this file, then colours its code into `source.coloured.json`, and the server reads the coloured book once, when it starts. The build that renders the pages ahead reads it the same way. The book changes only when the site is built again, so there is nothing to gain from reading it per request, and two megabytes of JSON are worth parsing only once.

The server parses the file and decodes it against the model's `Corpus` schema, which also drops what the shell never draws, such as where each block came from in its loom. A file that is missing or does not decode leaves the server without a book. It still serves every other page, and the source page's addresses then name no page.

=>

const bookFile = join(here, 'source.coloured.json')

const decodeBook = S.decodeUnknownOption(Corpus)

export const readBook = (fs: FileSystem.FileSystem): Effect.Effect<Option.Option<Corpus>> =>
  fs.readFileString(bookFile).pipe(
    Effect.map((text) =>
      Option.flatMap(Option.liftThrowable((json: string): unknown => JSON.parse(json))(text), decodeBook),
    ),
    Effect.catchCause(() => Effect.succeed(Option.none())),
  )

## Rendering one page

One model renders one page. The server and the build hand Foldkit the same three things the client boots from: the model's schema as the shape of the flags, an `init` that returns the model it is given, and the view. `Server.renderToString` runs the view over that model and returns the body and the title, with the model serialized beside the body. `Server.injectIntoTemplate` then writes both into the shell, the body over the empty `#root` and the title over the shell's own.

=>

const application = {
  Flags: Model,
  init: (model: Model) => ({ model }),
  view,
}

~

Foldkit stamps the page with a build id, and the client refuses a page whose id differs from its own. The id is the path of the entry script the shell loads. Vite names that file after a hash of its contents, so the path changes with every build that changes the client, and the client reads the same path from its own address. A shell without an entry script is not a build the server can serve, so the render dies rather than stamping a page no client could ever boot.

=>

const ENTRY = /<script type="module"[^>]*\ssrc="([^"]+)"/

export const entryOf = (shell: string): Effect.Effect<string> =>
  Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(ENTRY.exec(shell)?.[1]), {
    onNone: () => Effect.die(new Error('the shell loads no entry script')),
    onSome: Effect.succeed,
  })

export const pageOf = (shell: string, model: Model): Effect.Effect<string> =>
  Effect.gen(function* () {
    const buildId = yield* entryOf(shell)
    const rendered = yield* Server.renderToString(application, { flags: model, buildId })
    return Server.injectIntoTemplate(shell, rendered)
  }).pipe(Effect.orDie)

~

The search index reads the site's own pages as their rendered bodies, so `bodyOf` renders a route's page and hands back only its body. That body never reaches a browser, so it needs no shell, and any build id will do.

=>

export const bodyOf = (route: Route): Effect.Effect<string> =>
  Server.renderToString(application, { flags: { ...seed, route }, buildId: 'search' }).pipe(
    Effect.map((rendered) => rendered.html),
    Effect.orDie,
  )

## Every page the site answers

A path names a page in one of three ways. It is a route's own path, such as `/why-loom` or `/docs`, which opens the route as it stands. Or it is a chapter's address under `/docs`, which opens the documentation on that chapter. Or it is a chapter's address under `/source`, which opens the book on that chapter, when there is a book. `/source` itself opens the book's first chapter, so it too names a page only when there is a book. Without one it would render the page's frame around nothing: the nav, an empty column and the footer. The `placeOf` function reads a path as the place it names, or as no place at all.

=>

export type Place = { readonly route: Route; readonly chapter: string }

export const placeOf = (book: Option.Option<Corpus>, pathname: string): Option.Option<Place> =>
  pipe(
    routeOf(pathname),
    Option.filter((route) => route !== 'source' || Option.isSome(book)),
    Option.map((route): Place => ({ route, chapter: '' })),
    Option.orElse(() =>
      Option.map(docsChapterAt(pathname), (chapter): Place => ({ route: 'docs', chapter })),
    ),
    Option.orElse(() =>
      Option.flatMap(book, (corpus) =>
        Option.map(
          chapterAt(corpus, '/source', pathname),
          (chapter): Place => ({ route: 'source', chapter }),
        ),
      ),
    ),
  )

~

A page's model is the seed with its place written over it. A source page also carries its slice of the book: the book's table of contents and the one chapter the place names, with its code already in colour. Every other page carries none. The `modelAt` function builds the model for a place, for the server and the build alike.

=>

const bookFor = (book: Option.Option<Corpus>, place: Place): Option.Option<Corpus> =>
  place.route === 'source'
    ? Option.map(book, (corpus) => sliceOf(corpus, place.chapter))
    : Option.none()

export const modelAt = (book: Option.Option<Corpus>, place: Place): Model => ({
  ...seed,
  ...place,
  book: bookFor(book, place),
})

~

The build needs the same places the other way round, as every address the site answers. The `everyAddress` function lists them: each route's own path, the address of every chapter of the documentation, and the address of every chapter of the book.

=>

export const everyAddress = (book: Option.Option<Corpus>): ReadonlyArray<string> => [
  ...Array.map(Route.literals, pathOf),
  ...Array.map(docsCorpus.pages, (page) => chapterPath('/docs', page.slug)),
  ...Option.match(book, {
    onNone: () => [],
    onSome: (corpus) => Array.map(corpus.pages, (page) => chapterPath('/source', page.slug)),
  }),
]

## Where a page rendered ahead is kept

The build keeps each page it renders as a file under `dist/pages`, in a folder named after the build. A build's name is its id, the entry script the shell loads, without its folder or its extension, such as `index-DJidxQvj`. Inside that folder the page for an address sits at the address with `.html` added, and the landing sits at `index.html`.

The folder's name is what keeps a page rendered ahead honest. A page names the bundle and the stylesheet of the shell it was rendered into, so it is good only while the shell still names the same build. A new build has a new name, and until it renders its own pages the server finds none kept for it.

=>

export const distDir = join(here, '..', 'dist')

export const PAGES_DIR = join(distDir, 'pages')

export const pageFileOf = (buildId: string, address: string): string =>
  join(
    PAGES_DIR,
    basename(buildId, extname(buildId)),
    `${address === '/' ? 'index' : address.slice(1)}.html`,
  )

## The pages module {Tangle}[pages.ts]

The pages module draws on `effect`, on Node's `path` and `url` helpers to find its files, and on Foldkit's experimental `Server` module, which renders a view to HTML and writes it into the shell. The site's own modules supply the rest. The view draws the page. The model lends the `Model` schema, which doubles as the shape of the flags a page carries, the `Corpus` schema the book is read against, the `Route` schema that lists every route, and the `pathOf` and `routeOf` functions, which turn a route into its path and a path into its route. The documentation shell lends the documentation itself, as `docsCorpus`, and the functions that read and write a chapter's address and cut the slice of a corpus one page shows.

=>

import { Array, Effect, FileSystem, Option, pipe, Schema as S } from 'effect'
import { basename, dirname, extname, join } from 'node:path'
import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url'
import { Server } from 'foldkit/experimental'
import { view } from './view'
import { Corpus, Model, pathOf, Route, routeOf } from './model'
import { chapterAt, chapterPath, docsChapterAt, docsCorpus, sliceOf } from './docs-page'

const here = dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url))

::[The page]

::[Loom's own book]

