## What the server draws on

From `effect` come `Effect`, the `Cache` that keeps each page the server renders itself, the `FileSystem` that reads the shell and the pages rendered ahead, the `Option` that carries a path naming no page, the `Schema` that encodes a search's answer, and the `Context` and `Layer` that define the server and wire it. From `effect/http` come the request and the responses. `@effect/platform-bun` supplies the Bun HTTP server, its file services, and the runtime edge.

The pages module lends everything a page needs: the book, the places a path can name, a place's model, the render, and the file where a page rendered ahead is kept. The model lends the `Route` schema, which lists every route the site has, and the `SearchGroup` schema a search answers in. The documentation shell lends the documentation itself, as `docsCorpus`, which the cache counts its chapters from. The search module lends `indexOf`, which builds the index the palette searches, `searchIn`, which answers a query by its words, and `groupsOf`, which gathers any search's results by page. The meaning module lends `Meaning`, which answers a query by its meaning.

The client never reads an address itself. It boots from the model the page carries, so the server and the client always agree on which page and which chapter are on show.

=>

import { Cache, Context, Effect, FileSystem, Layer, Option, Schema as S } from 'effect'
import { HttpServer, HttpServerRequest, HttpServerResponse } from 'effect/http'
import { BunHttpServer, BunRuntime, BunServices } from '@effect/platform-bun'
import { join } from 'node:path'
import { Route, SearchGroup } from './model'
import { docsCorpus } from './docs-page'
import { type Entry, groupsOf, indexOf, searchIn } from './search'
import { Meaning } from './meaning'
import {
  bodyOf,
  distDir,
  entryOf,
  modelAt,
  pageFileOf,
  pageOf,
  placeOf,
  readBook,
  type Place,
} from './pages'

## The pages the server writes itself

Two answers are pages the server writes on its own: the one for an address the site does not have, and the one for a `dist` that has not been built yet.

Neither is built on the shell. The missing page cannot be, because the shell boots the client, and the client hydrates only a page the server rendered for it. Handed a shell with no model in it, Foldkit refuses the page and locks it. The unbuilt page cannot be either, for the plainer reason that the shell is the very thing absent.

So they stand alone and share a shape — centred on the site's paper, set in the site's type, one line of prose and nothing waiting on a bundle.

They carry no theme switch, so they follow the device. A device set to dark gets the site's slate ground and cool ink, and the page declares both themes so the browser does not darken it on its own. Their tab wears the site's icon, the same emblem the application's page names.

=>

const PAGE_STYLE = `
      :root { color-scheme: light dark; --paper: #F7F4EC; --ink: #14130F; --ink-3: #7C6F64; --rule: #E4DCCB; }
      @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
        :root { --paper: #141821; --ink: #E6E9EF; --ink-3: #8790A3; --rule: #2F384A; }
      }
      body {
        margin: 0;
        min-height: 100vh;
        display: flex;
        flex-direction: column;
        align-items: center;
        justify-content: center;
        gap: 24px;
        padding: 40px;
        background: var(--paper);
        color: var(--ink);
        font-family: 'Lilex', 'IBM Plex Mono', ui-monospace, monospace;
        text-align: center;
      }
      .number { font-size: 46px; letter-spacing: 0.02em; }
      .headline { font-size: 24px; letter-spacing: 0.01em; }
      .line { margin: 0; max-width: 52ch; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--ink-3); }
      .line code { color: var(--ink); }
      .back {
        color: var(--ink);
        text-decoration: none;
        padding-bottom: 3px;
        border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
      }
      .back:hover { color: var(--ink-3); }
`

const standalone = (title: string, body: string): string => `<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>${title}</title>
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/languages/loom.svg" />
    <style>${PAGE_STYLE}</style>
  </head>
  <body>${body}
  </body>
</html>
`

~

The missing page carries the mark, the number, a sentence, and the way back. A reader who has just missed a page waits on nothing to be told so, and the same page answers in development and in production alike. The mark wears its dark face when the device is dark, through a picture source that matches the same setting the page's colours do.

=>

const MISSING = standalone(
  'Not found — Loom',
  `
    <picture>
      <source srcset="/mark-dark.svg" media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)" />
      <img src="/mark.svg" alt="Loom" width="140" height="60" />
    </picture>
    <div class="number">404</div>
    <p class="line">There is no page at this address.</p>
    <a class="back" href="/">Back to Loom</a>`,
)

const notFound = Effect.succeed(
  HttpServerResponse.text(MISSING, { status: 404, contentType: 'text/html' }),
)

~

The unbuilt page carries no mark, since the mark is one of the files that has not been written. It names the command that fixes the matter, which costs a line and saves the reader working out why a directory is empty.

=>

const NOT_BUILT = standalone(
  'Not built — Loom',
  `
    <div class="headline">The site is not built</div>
    <p class="line">The server renders from <code>dist</code>, and nothing is there yet. Run <code>bun run build</code>, or <code>bun run dev</code> to keep it building.</p>`,
)

## Read fresh, rendered ahead

The site reads its shell for each page rather than holding one from startup, because `dist` is rewritten underneath a running server every time a build finishes. A shell held from startup would go on naming the bundle the first build wrote. The page would then arrive running code a developer has since changed, or pointing at files a later build has removed. Reading it per request costs one small file and keeps the server honest about whatever `dist` holds at that moment.

Two things are made once rather than per request: the book and the search index. Both change only with a build. The server waits only for the book before it is ready to answer. Every request needs the book, since the book decides which `/source` addresses name a page, and reading it takes a few tens of milliseconds.

The server leaves the search index for last. It builds the index when the first search asks for it, and the `Effect.cached` combinator keeps the index for every search after. Building the index occupies the server for most of a second, and no other request is answered in that time. A development server restarts on every tangle, and an index built at startup would stall the very pages a developer reloads after a change.

Two answers are the whole of what the site owes the handler. The `pageAt` function asks `placeOf` whether the path names a page at all, then answers with that page in the shell it has just read. Any other path names no page, so the server looks for an asset, and answers with the missing page when there is none. A `dist` it cannot read yields the unbuilt page. A render that fails is a fault in the site rather than in the build, so it surfaces as one instead of hiding behind the unbuilt page. The `assetAt` function reads a file from `dist` — the client bundle, the stylesheet, the marks — and falls back to the missing page when there is no such file. Both take the request's whole URL, since a path alone is what the handler happens to match on rather than all it holds.

A page comes from one of two places. The `pageIn` function first looks for the page rendered ahead for the shell's build, and answers with that file when the build has written it. Otherwise it renders the page itself. That happens in development, where every rebuild is a new build that renders nothing ahead, and in production only for a page the build left out.

A page the server renders, it keeps. Rendering one place twice through one shell gives the same HTML twice, since a page's model is fixed once the server has read the book. So the `rendered` cache renders a page the first time a request asks for it, and keeps the finished HTML for every request after.

The cache keys each page by its place and by the shell it was written into. The shell is what keeps a kept page honest. A build that changes the bundle or the stylesheet writes their new names into the shell, and every page then has a new key. Each page renders afresh the first time a request asks for it, and no page written into an older shell is served again.

The cache has room for one page at each address the site answers. All of those pages together come to about twelve megabytes of HTML. When a build changes the shell, the pages written into the new shell push out those written into the old one, the least recently asked first. Two requests that ask for the same page at once share one render.

The unbuilt page stays out of the cache. It answers only when there is no shell to key it by, and it is a constant with nothing to render.

The site answers one more thing, a search. `searchAt` reads the query from the request's `q` parameter and answers with groups of results, encoded through the `SearchGroup` schema, so the client reads back exactly the shape the server meant. It searches by meaning first, through the `Meaning` service that Part XII describes, and by words when meaning has no answer. A request whose `exact` parameter is `1` asks for words alone, and `searchIn` answers it straight away. The `from` parameter names the route the reader searches from, which decides the order the pages come in. A request that names no route, or one the site does not have, is ordered as the landing would be.

=>

type PageKey = { readonly place: Place; readonly shell: string }

const decodeRoute = S.decodeUnknownOption(Route)

export class Site extends Context.Service<Site>()('Site', {
  make: Effect.gen(function* () {
    const fs = yield* FileSystem.FileSystem
    const meaning = yield* Meaning
    const book = yield* readBook(fs)
    const bookChapters = Option.match(book, {
      onNone: () => 0,
      onSome: (corpus) => corpus.pages.length,
    })
    const index = yield* Effect.cached(indexOf(book, bodyOf))
    const rendered = yield* Cache.make({
      capacity: Route.literals.length + docsCorpus.pages.length + bookChapters,
      lookup: (key: PageKey) => pageOf(key.shell, modelAt(book, key.place)),
    })
    const pageIn = (shell: string, place: Place, address: string): Effect.Effect<string> =>
      Effect.flatMap(entryOf(shell), (buildId) =>
        fs
          .readFileString(pageFileOf(buildId, address))
          .pipe(Effect.catchCause(() => Cache.get(rendered, { place, shell }))),
      )
    const shell = fs.readFileString(join(distDir, 'index.html'))
    return {
      pageAt: (url: URL): Effect.Effect<Option.Option<string>> =>
        Option.match(placeOf(book, url.pathname), {
          onNone: () => Effect.succeed(Option.none()),
          onSome: (place) =>
            shell.pipe(
              Effect.matchEffect({
                onFailure: () => Effect.succeed(NOT_BUILT),
                onSuccess: (held) => pageIn(held, place, url.pathname),
              }),
              Effect.map(Option.some),
            ),
        }),
      assetAt: (url: URL) =>
        HttpServerResponse.file(join(distDir, url.pathname)).pipe(
          Effect.catchCause(() => notFound),
        ),
      searchAt: (url: URL): Effect.Effect<HttpServerResponse.HttpServerResponse> => {
        const query = url.searchParams.get('q') ?? ''
        const from = Option.getOrElse(decodeRoute(url.searchParams.get('from')), (): Route => 'home')
        const byWords = (entries: ReadonlyArray<Entry>) => Effect.succeed(searchIn(entries, query, from))
        const byMeaning = (entries: ReadonlyArray<Entry>) =>
          Effect.map(
            meaning.rank(entries, query),
            Option.match({
              onNone: () => searchIn(entries, query, from),
              onSome: (results) => groupsOf(results, from),
            }),
          )
        return Effect.flatMap(index, (entries) =>
          url.searchParams.get('exact') === '1' ? byWords(entries) : byMeaning(entries),
        ).pipe(
          Effect.flatMap(HttpServerResponse.schemaJson(S.Array(SearchGroup))),
          Effect.orDie,
        )
      },
    } as const
  }),
}) {
  static readonly layer = Layer.effect(this, this.make).pipe(
    Layer.provide(BunServices.layer),
    Layer.provide(Meaning.layer),
  )
}

## Answering a request

The server answers `/search` from the index. For any other path it asks the site for a page at the request URL. A path that names a route returns it as HTML, and anything else is an asset read from `dist`.

=>

const SEARCH = '/search'

const handle = (site: Site['Service'], url: URL) =>
  url.pathname === SEARCH
    ? site.searchAt(url)
    : Effect.flatMap(
        site.pageAt(url),
        Option.match({
          onSome: (page) => Effect.succeed(HttpServerResponse.text(page, { contentType: 'text/html' })),
          onNone: () => site.assetAt(url),
        }),
      )

## Serving it {Tangle}[server.ts]

The handler reads the request and answers it. The server serves that handler over a Bun HTTP layer and launches from the Bun runtime edge. There is nothing here to switch on, which is the point: this file is what a developer runs and what the image runs, and neither reads an environment variable to find out which it is.

The order of the site's layer and the Bun HTTP layer decides when the port opens. The `Layer.provide` function builds the layer it is given before the layer it feeds, so the site, provided last, is built first. The Bun HTTP layer opens the port only after that, once the site can answer. Until the handler is installed, the Bun HTTP layer answers every request itself, with a bare `404` whose body reads `not found`. In the other order that placeholder would answer every request for as long as the site took to start, and a reload during a development restart would show an all but empty page.

=>

::[What the server draws on]

::[The pages the server writes itself]

::[Read fresh, rendered ahead]

::[Answering a request]

const app = Effect.gen(function* () {
  const request = yield* HttpServerRequest.HttpServerRequest
  const site = yield* Site
  return yield* handle(site, new URL(request.url, 'http://localhost'))
})

const port = Number(process.env.PORT ?? 5199)

const server = HttpServer.serve(app).pipe(
  HttpServer.withLogAddress,
  Layer.provide(BunHttpServer.layer({ port })),
  Layer.provide(Site.layer),
)

BunRuntime.runMain(Layer.launch(server))
